{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795137742747,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"partID":"prt_1f68c2649001Tb7IDRLXhy25Er","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f68c1c2e001VFyWAhlGVtzaZE","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a68a56cd90c0ae57","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -rn \"8-11\\|8–11\\|setup.*minute\\|minutes.*setup\" specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/description.json specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/goal.md specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/implementation-summary.md 2>/dev/null | head"},"output":"(no output)","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"(no output)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -rn \\\"8-11\\\\|8–11\\\\|setup.*minute\\\\|minutes.*setup\\\" specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/description.json specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/goal.md specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/implementation-summary.md 2>/dev/null | head\"}"},"time":{"start":1795137742409,"end":1795137742747}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795137746012,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"partID":"prt_1f68c25470025yP1I3Qpa67LKD","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f68c1c2e001VFyWAhlGVtzaZE","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a5b5a5cd86bac10f","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -rn \"8-11\\|8–11\\|8 to 11\\|11 minutes\\|eight minute\\|minutes of setup\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop .skilled/commands/deep specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode 2>/dev/null | head -20"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/changelog/v1.15.13.0.md:3:description: \"v1.15.13.0 makes maxToolCallsPerIteration in the run config the only deep-research iteration budget. The agent, the workflow YAMLs, SKILL.md and the loop protocol stop restating 8-11, 12 or 24, and point at the Max N line the iteration prompt renders.\"\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/manual-testing-playbook/entry-points-and-modes/live-extend-expansive-research-run.md:34:- Observed cost: about 11 minutes; 0.33M input, 0.03M output, 0.03M reasoning and 6.2M cache-read tokens for the one added iteration.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/node_modules/prebuild-install/CHANGELOG.md:41:## [6.1.4] - 2021-08-11\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/node_modules/better-sqlite3/deps/sqlite3/sqlite3.c:203:** on 2008-11-28.) These days, all Linux kernels support large files, so\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/feature-catalog/loop-lifecycle/expansive-depth-mode.md:30:The active node's next uncovered dimension is dispatched, apart from the last iteration under a cap of 2 or more while a core traceability protocol is unfinished and `traceability` is among the run's dimensions, which reviews `traceability`. A run whose `reviewDimensions` list leaves traceability out is not steered to it, so its last iteration reviews the configured set. A node needs one iteration per dimension in its universe, the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the configured `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name. Only an evidence-bearing attempt counts as a frontier visit: `complete`, `insight`, `stuck` or a legacy row with no status. A `thought`, `error` or `timeout` record covers no dimension, spends no band share and does not count for the focus tie-break. Each depth band starts from its `budgetSplit` share of `--max-iterations` (0.5, 0.35 and 0.15), plus any share an earlier band left unspent once that band holds no open node, so set `--max-iterations` to at least `2 * ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions`, which gives the depth-1 band `ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions` iterations. A store row that does not parse is skipped, and the reducer reports it on stderr and in the frontier projection's `warnings` with its file and line. The run-wide `maxFrontierNodes` cap defaults to half of `maxIterations`, and every seed that is not pruned counts toward it. `--frontier-max-depth=N` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` set `maxDepth` and `maxFrontierNodes` when the config is created; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the config's `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name it adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below three, the ceiling of 24 binds from the second seed at three and from the first at four or more, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/changelog/v1.11.15.0.md:3:description: \"v1.11.15.0 raises the deep-review iteration budget to 24 calls in the run config and renders it into the iteration prompt as a Max N line. The agent's three budget profiles, the YAMLs' unread tool_call_budget and the restated 8-11 and 12 limits are gone.\"\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md:464:Under `--depth-mode=expansive` the loop keeps a frontier of sub-target nodes beside the review target. The flag writes `antiConvergence.depthMode: \"expansive\"` and the caps in `expansiveDepth`: `maxDepth` 3, `maxChildrenPerNode` 6, a review root up to 8 depth-1 children because the seed asks for 3 to 8 sub-targets, a `budgetSplit` of 0.5/0.35/0.15 across the depth bands and `maxFrontierNodes`, which defaults to half of `maxIterations` unless the config sets a number. Two flags set the caps when the config is created: `--frontier-max-depth=N` writes `maxDepth` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` writes `maxFrontierNodes`; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. The depth-1 seed answers only to the root-children cap, but every seed that is not pruned counts toward the run cap. The depth-1 band gets only `budgetSplit.depth1` of `maxIterations`, so fund it with at least `2 * ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions` iterations. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the config's `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name it adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below three, the ceiling of 24 binds from the second seed at three and from the first at four or more, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap. A run without the flag keeps the default path and runs exactly as before, and the mode is locked against the optimizer; the `expansiveDepth` caps are in neither its tunable nor its locked list.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/convergence/convergence.md:105:`--depth-mode=expansive` grows a depth-capped frontier of sub-target nodes beside the review target. The value lands in `antiConvergence.depthMode`, and the caps live in `expansiveDepth`: `maxDepth` 3, `maxChildrenPerNode` 6, a review root up to 8 depth-1 children because the seed asks for 3 to 8 sub-targets, a `budgetSplit` of 0.5/0.35/0.15 across the depth bands and `maxFrontierNodes`, which defaults to half of `maxIterations` unless the config sets a number. Two flags set the caps when the config is created: `--frontier-max-depth=N` writes `maxDepth` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` writes `maxFrontierNodes`; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. Once a band's spend reaches its `budgetSplit` share of `maxIterations`, the reducer closes every open or active node in that band as `exhausted` with reason `budget`, and an unspent share rolls down to the next band only after its own band holds no open node. The run cap never prunes a depth-1 seed, which only the root-children cap bounds, but every seed that is not pruned, answered ones included, counts toward the live total that later admissions are checked against. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the config's `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name it adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below three, the ceiling of 24 binds from the second seed at three and from the first at four or more, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap. A fan-out parent seeds before any config exists, so its cap falls back to 10. The mode is locked against the optimizer; the `expansiveDepth` block is in neither its tunable nor its locked list. An absent flag means `default`, which runs exactly as before.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/README.md:170:The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value. The seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/fanout-research-topic.txt:11:Q4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/frontier-nodes.jsonl:6:{\"nodeId\":\"F-1-006\",\"parentId\":\"root\",\"depth\":1,\"scope\":\"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review, specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review-archive\",\"question\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"status\":\"open\",\"reason\":null,\"iteration\":0,\"dedupeKey\":\"cost and latency what do the 8 11 minute setup the cost per iteration the 24 iteration ceiling and the 50 35 15 depth band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder s review and review archive and the live playbook logs and which change cuts the most time or money\"}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/inbox.jsonl:6:{\"id\":\"F-1-006\",\"text\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"source\":\"frontier\",\"origin\":\"frontier\",\"injectedAtIteration\":0}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/fanout-research-topic.txt:11:Q4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/frontier-nodes.jsonl:6:{\"nodeId\":\"F-1-006\",\"parentId\":\"root\",\"depth\":1,\"scope\":\"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review, specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review-archive\",\"question\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"status\":\"open\",\"reason\":null,\"iteration\":0,\"dedupeKey\":\"cost and latency what do the 8 11 minute setup the cost per iteration the 24 iteration ceiling and the 50 35 15 depth band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder s review and review archive and the live playbook logs and which change cuts the most time or money\"}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/inbox.jsonl:6:{\"id\":\"F-1-006\",\"text\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"source\":\"frontier\",\"origin\":\"frontier\",\"injectedAtIteration\":0}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/frontier-seed.json:28:    \"question\": \"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research_archive/20261120T011847Z/findings-registry.json:399:      \"text\": \"**A pivot only fires on a clean eligible STOP behind a long conjunction — and this run's own config disables it twice over.** The workflow branch runs only when `convergence_mode == 'divergent'` AND `stop_decision.reasonDetail ∈ {composite_converged, all_questions_answered}` [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1056]. The stop must first survive every earlier return in `convergence.cjs`: `maxIterationsReached` (terminal, :1115), `consecutiveFailures` (:1126), `stuckThresholdReached` (:1130), `convergenceMode 'off'` → CONTINUE (:1134), **`stopPolicy 'max-iterations'` → CONTINUE before any stop candidate is even formed (:1138–1140)**, `minIterationsNotReached` (:1159), and `qualityGuardFailed` → CONTINUE (:1167–1168). A graph-vetoed `blockedStop` and every non-eligible reason fail closed to the terminal path (yaml:1061). This packet's config sets `stopPolicy: 'max-iterations'` (config:97) and `convergenceMode: 'default'` (config:7), so the pivot path is unreachable here twice — once at the mode gate, once before a candidate exists. Generation 1's \\\"existing levers add duration only\\\" is confirmed mechanically: `max-iterations` doesn't just decline to stop early, it structurally removes the only signal that could trigger a pivot.\",\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research_archive/20261120T011847Z/iterations/iteration-002.md:15:1. **A pivot only fires on a clean eligible STOP behind a long conjunction — and this run's own config disables it twice over.** The workflow branch runs only when `convergence_mode == 'divergent'` AND `stop_decision.reasonDetail ∈ {composite_converged, all_questions_answered}` [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1056]. The stop must first survive every earlier return in `convergence.cjs`: `maxIterationsReached` (terminal, :1115), `consecutiveFailures` (:1126), `stuckThresholdReached` (:1130), `convergenceMode 'off'` → CONTINUE (:1134), **`stopPolicy 'max-iterations'` → CONTINUE before any stop candidate is even formed (:1138–1140)**, `minIterationsNotReached` (:1159), and `qualityGuardFailed` → CONTINUE (:1167–1168). A graph-vetoed `blockedStop` and every non-eligible reason fail closed to the terminal path (yaml:1061). This packet's config sets `stopPolicy: 'max-iterations'` (config:97) and `convergenceMode: 'default'` (config:7), so the pivot path is unreachable here twice — once at the mode gate, once before a candidate exists. Generation 1's \"existing levers add duration only\" is confirmed mechanically: `max-iterations` doesn't just decline to stop early, it structurally removes the only signal that could trigger a pivot.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/deep-research-state.jsonl:1:{\"type\":\"config\",\"topic\":\"What changes would make the expansive-depth deep-loop modes (research and review), and the deep-loop runtime behind them, simpler, cheaper, easier to operate and identical across CLI runtimes, ranked by value over effort?\\n\\nRead the code at HEAD d4420e9510d. The spec folder is specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode (SPEC below). Its fan-out review (SPEC/review/review-report.md) and the fix commits a424fb6cd8a..d4420e9510d already closed that review's findings: build on them and do not re-report them. Every recommendation names one concrete change, cites file:line (or a source URL for prior art), and states value, effort (S/M/L) and risk.\\n\\nQ1 Restraint: what can be deleted, merged or collapsed across runtime/scripts/frontier-admit.cjs, both reduce-state.cjs reducers, runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs, runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs and the two workflow YAMLs (.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml and deep-review.yaml) while every promise stays kept? Name concrete deletions.\\n\\nQ2 Operator UX: flag discoverability, defaults, budget sizing, warnings, dashboards, reports, the dry-run preview, and recovery mid-run (provider quota, resume, extend, rerunning one failed lineage).\\n\\nQ3 Cross-CLI parity: where cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-codex, cli-pi, cli-devin, cli-cursor, cli-deepseek-harness and cli-mistral-vibe still differ in permissions, effort flags, failure classes, resume and log shape (fanout-run.cjs LINEAGE_COMMAND_ADAPTERS, runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts). What single contract would remove the differences?\\n\\nQ4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\\n\\nQ5 Open design questions. (a) Should research count stuck passes per frontier node, as review now does? Research still counts lineage-wide at deep-research.yaml:1488 and keeps its own node-marker regex. (b) The lineage completion check (fanout-run.cjs TERMINAL_STOP_EVENTS_BY_LOOP_TYPE and checkLineageCompletion) marks 3 of 12 older committed research lineages as failed, because they predate the closeout row. Is that a backward-compatibility break for resume and extend, and what is the right rule? (c) Pivot admission into the frontier was dropped for structural reasons (D-011-10 in SPEC/011-review-frontier-mode/decision-record.md). Is there a cheap design that makes it work?\\n\\nQ6 Prior art: how other agentic research and code-review systems handle frontier growth, stuck detection, stop rules and multi-executor fan-out. This is the only question that needs the web; cite source URLs, and never put personal data such as an email address in a search query or request.\\n\\nReport only: change no code or doc outside your own lineage directory.\",\"maxIterations\":10,\"generation\":1,\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-20T01:21:07.705Z\"}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/deep-research-dashboard.md:28:Q4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/changelog/v1.15.13.0.md:3:description: \"v1.15.13.0 makes maxToolCallsPerIteration in the run config the only deep-research iteration budget. The agent, the workflow YAMLs, SKILL.md and the loop protocol stop restating 8-11, 12 or 24, and point at the Max N line the iteration prompt renders.\"\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/manual-testing-playbook/entry-points-and-modes/live-extend-expansive-research-run.md:34:- Observed cost: about 11 minutes; 0.33M input, 0.03M output, 0.03M reasoning and 6.2M cache-read tokens for the one added iteration.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/node_modules/prebuild-install/CHANGELOG.md:41:## [6.1.4] - 2021-08-11\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/node_modules/better-sqlite3/deps/sqlite3/sqlite3.c:203:** on 2008-11-28.) These days, all Linux kernels support large files, so\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/feature-catalog/loop-lifecycle/expansive-depth-mode.md:30:The active node's next uncovered dimension is dispatched, apart from the last iteration under a cap of 2 or more while a core traceability protocol is unfinished and `traceability` is among the run's dimensions, which reviews `traceability`. A run whose `reviewDimensions` list leaves traceability out is not steered to it, so its last iteration reviews the configured set. A node needs one iteration per dimension in its universe, the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the configured `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name. Only an evidence-bearing attempt counts as a frontier visit: `complete`, `insight`, `stuck` or a legacy row with no status. A `thought`, `error` or `timeout` record covers no dimension, spends no band share and does not count for the focus tie-break. Each depth band starts from its `budgetSplit` share of `--max-iterations` (0.5, 0.35 and 0.15), plus any share an earlier band left unspent once that band holds no open node, so set `--max-iterations` to at least `2 * ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions`, which gives the depth-1 band `ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions` iterations. A store row that does not parse is skipped, and the reducer reports it on stderr and in the frontier projection's `warnings` with its file and line. The run-wide `maxFrontierNodes` cap defaults to half of `maxIterations`, and every seed that is not pruned counts toward it. `--frontier-max-depth=N` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` set `maxDepth` and `maxFrontierNodes` when the config is created; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the config's `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name it adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below three, the ceiling of 24 binds from the second seed at three and from the first at four or more, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/changelog/v1.11.15.0.md:3:description: \"v1.11.15.0 raises the deep-review iteration budget to 24 calls in the run config and renders it into the iteration prompt as a Max N line. The agent's three budget profiles, the YAMLs' unread tool_call_budget and the restated 8-11 and 12 limits are gone.\"\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md:464:Under `--depth-mode=expansive` the loop keeps a frontier of sub-target nodes beside the review target. The flag writes `antiConvergence.depthMode: \"expansive\"` and the caps in `expansiveDepth`: `maxDepth` 3, `maxChildrenPerNode` 6, a review root up to 8 depth-1 children because the seed asks for 3 to 8 sub-targets, a `budgetSplit` of 0.5/0.35/0.15 across the depth bands and `maxFrontierNodes`, which defaults to half of `maxIterations` unless the config sets a number. Two flags set the caps when the config is created: `--frontier-max-depth=N` writes `maxDepth` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` writes `maxFrontierNodes`; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. The depth-1 seed answers only to the root-children cap, but every seed that is not pruned counts toward the run cap. The depth-1 band gets only `budgetSplit.depth1` of `maxIterations`, so fund it with at least `2 * ceil(nodes / lineages) * dimensions` iterations. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the config's `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name it adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below three, the ceiling of 24 binds from the second seed at three and from the first at four or more, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap. A run without the flag keeps the default path and runs exactly as before, and the mode is locked against the optimizer; the `expansiveDepth` caps are in neither its tunable nor its locked list.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/convergence/convergence.md:105:`--depth-mode=expansive` grows a depth-capped frontier of sub-target nodes beside the review target. The value lands in `antiConvergence.depthMode`, and the caps live in `expansiveDepth`: `maxDepth` 3, `maxChildrenPerNode` 6, a review root up to 8 depth-1 children because the seed asks for 3 to 8 sub-targets, a `budgetSplit` of 0.5/0.35/0.15 across the depth bands and `maxFrontierNodes`, which defaults to half of `maxIterations` unless the config sets a number. Two flags set the caps when the config is created: `--frontier-max-depth=N` writes `maxDepth` and `--frontier-max-nodes=N` writes `maxFrontierNodes`; both take a positive integer, need `--depth-mode=expansive` on a single-executor run, and are refused on a fan-out config. Once a band's spend reaches its `budgetSplit` share of `maxIterations`, the reducer closes every open or active node in that band as `exhausted` with reason `budget`, and an unspent share rolls down to the next band only after its own band holds no open node. The run cap never prunes a depth-1 seed, which only the root-children cap bounds, but every seed that is not pruned, answered ones included, counts toward the live total that later admissions are checked against. When `--max-iterations` is left at its default, the run sizes its budget from the seed: `min(24, max(7, 2 × D × (S + maxDepth − 1)))` iterations for S seeded depth-1 nodes and D the four canonical dimensions less traceability when the config's `reviewDimensions` list leaves it out, plus each custom name it adds, each counted once, and the seed step prints the derived budget. Since D is never below three, the ceiling of 24 binds from the second seed at three and from the first at four or more, so one run cannot fund every node at every depth of a wide review; split a wide review with fan-out, where each lineage with no matrix `iterations` value sizes its own budget from its share of the seed, with D fixed at the canonical four because a lineage reviews only those. A lineage dealt no seed node, or a run whose seeds were all pruned, runs at its loop default instead: 12 iterations for a native lineage and 7 for a CLI one or a single run. The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value, and the seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap. A fan-out parent seeds before any config exists, so its cap falls back to 10. The mode is locked against the optimizer; the `expansiveDepth` block is in neither its tunable nor its locked list. An absent flag means `default`, which runs exactly as before.\n.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/README.md:170:The node cap stays half the budget, which leaves room for at least two nodes past depth 1. An explicit `--max-iterations`, marker, setup answer or fan-out matrix `iterations` value always wins, even below the derived value. The seed warns when it leaves no room past depth 1. At about seven to eight minutes an iteration, a 24-iteration review can reach `maxDurationMinutes` (120) before its iteration cap.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/fanout-research-topic.txt:11:Q4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/frontier-nodes.jsonl:6:{\"nodeId\":\"F-1-006\",\"parentId\":\"root\",\"depth\":1,\"scope\":\"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review, specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review-archive\",\"question\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"status\":\"open\",\"reason\":null,\"iteration\":0,\"dedupeKey\":\"cost and latency what do the 8 11 minute setup the cost per iteration the 24 iteration ceiling and the 50 35 15 depth band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder s review and review archive and the live playbook logs and which change cuts the most time or money\"}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/inbox.jsonl:6:{\"id\":\"F-1-006\",\"text\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"source\":\"frontier\",\"origin\":\"frontier\",\"injectedAtIteration\":0}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/fanout-research-topic.txt:11:Q4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/frontier-nodes.jsonl:6:{\"nodeId\":\"F-1-006\",\"parentId\":\"root\",\"depth\":1,\"scope\":\"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review, specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/review-archive\",\"question\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"status\":\"open\",\"reason\":null,\"iteration\":0,\"dedupeKey\":\"cost and latency what do the 8 11 minute setup the cost per iteration the 24 iteration ceiling and the 50 35 15 depth band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder s review and review archive and the live playbook logs and which change cuts the most time or money\"}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/inbox.jsonl:6:{\"id\":\"F-1-006\",\"text\":\"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\"source\":\"frontier\",\"origin\":\"frontier\",\"injectedAtIteration\":0}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/frontier-seed.json:28:    \"question\": \"Cost and latency: what do the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split cost in the measured runs under the spec folder's review/ and review-archive/ and the live playbook logs, and which change cuts the most time or money?\",\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research_archive/20261120T011847Z/findings-registry.json:399:      \"text\": \"**A pivot only fires on a clean eligible STOP behind a long conjunction — and this run's own config disables it twice over.** The workflow branch runs only when `convergence_mode == 'divergent'` AND `stop_decision.reasonDetail ∈ {composite_converged, all_questions_answered}` [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1056]. The stop must first survive every earlier return in `convergence.cjs`: `maxIterationsReached` (terminal, :1115), `consecutiveFailures` (:1126), `stuckThresholdReached` (:1130), `convergenceMode 'off'` → CONTINUE (:1134), **`stopPolicy 'max-iterations'` → CONTINUE before any stop candidate is even formed (:1138–1140)**, `minIterationsNotReached` (:1159), and `qualityGuardFailed` → CONTINUE (:1167–1168). A graph-vetoed `blockedStop` and every non-eligible reason fail closed to the terminal path (yaml:1061). This packet's config sets `stopPolicy: 'max-iterations'` (config:97) and `convergenceMode: 'default'` (config:7), so the pivot path is unreachable here twice — once at the mode gate, once before a candidate exists. Generation 1's \\\"existing levers add duration only\\\" is confirmed mechanically: `max-iterations` doesn't just decline to stop early, it structurally removes the only signal that could trigger a pivot.\",\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/containment/baseline/specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research_archive/20261120T011847Z/iterations/iteration-002.md:15:1. **A pivot only fires on a clean eligible STOP behind a long conjunction — and this run's own config disables it twice over.** The workflow branch runs only when `convergence_mode == 'divergent'` AND `stop_decision.reasonDetail ∈ {composite_converged, all_questions_answered}` [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1056]. The stop must first survive every earlier return in `convergence.cjs`: `maxIterationsReached` (terminal, :1115), `consecutiveFailures` (:1126), `stuckThresholdReached` (:1130), `convergenceMode 'off'` → CONTINUE (:1134), **`stopPolicy 'max-iterations'` → CONTINUE before any stop candidate is even formed (:1138–1140)**, `minIterationsNotReached` (:1159), and `qualityGuardFailed` → CONTINUE (:1167–1168). A graph-vetoed `blockedStop` and every non-eligible reason fail closed to the terminal path (yaml:1061). This packet's config sets `stopPolicy: 'max-iterations'` (config:97) and `convergenceMode: 'default'` (config:7), so the pivot path is unreachable here twice — once at the mode gate, once before a candidate exists. Generation 1's \"existing levers add duration only\" is confirmed mechanically: `max-iterations` doesn't just decline to stop early, it structurally removes the only signal that could trigger a pivot.\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/deep-research-state.jsonl:1:{\"type\":\"config\",\"topic\":\"What changes would make the expansive-depth deep-loop modes (research and review), and the deep-loop runtime behind them, simpler, cheaper, easier to operate and identical across CLI runtimes, ranked by value over effort?\\n\\nRead the code at HEAD d4420e9510d. The spec folder is specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode (SPEC below). Its fan-out review (SPEC/review/review-report.md) and the fix commits a424fb6cd8a..d4420e9510d already closed that review's findings: build on them and do not re-report them. Every recommendation names one concrete change, cites file:line (or a source URL for prior art), and states value, effort (S/M/L) and risk.\\n\\nQ1 Restraint: what can be deleted, merged or collapsed across runtime/scripts/frontier-admit.cjs, both reduce-state.cjs reducers, runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs, runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs and the two workflow YAMLs (.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml and deep-review.yaml) while every promise stays kept? Name concrete deletions.\\n\\nQ2 Operator UX: flag discoverability, defaults, budget sizing, warnings, dashboards, reports, the dry-run preview, and recovery mid-run (provider quota, resume, extend, rerunning one failed lineage).\\n\\nQ3 Cross-CLI parity: where cli-claude-code, cli-opencode, cli-codex, cli-pi, cli-devin, cli-cursor, cli-deepseek-harness and cli-mistral-vibe still differ in permissions, effort flags, failure classes, resume and log shape (fanout-run.cjs LINEAGE_COMMAND_ADAPTERS, runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts). What single contract would remove the differences?\\n\\nQ4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\\n\\nQ5 Open design questions. (a) Should research count stuck passes per frontier node, as review now does? Research still counts lineage-wide at deep-research.yaml:1488 and keeps its own node-marker regex. (b) The lineage completion check (fanout-run.cjs TERMINAL_STOP_EVENTS_BY_LOOP_TYPE and checkLineageCompletion) marks 3 of 12 older committed research lineages as failed, because they predate the closeout row. Is that a backward-compatibility break for resume and extend, and what is the right rule? (c) Pivot admission into the frontier was dropped for structural reasons (D-011-10 in SPEC/011-review-frontier-mode/decision-record.md). Is there a cheap design that makes it work?\\n\\nQ6 Prior art: how other agentic research and code-review systems handle frontier growth, stuck detection, stop rules and multi-executor fan-out. This is the only question that needs the web; cite source URLs, and never put personal data such as an email address in a search query or request.\\n\\nReport only: change no code or doc outside your own lineage directory.\",\"maxIterations\":10,\"generation\":1,\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-20T01:21:07.705Z\"}\nspecs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/opus/deep-research-dashboard.md:28:Q4 Cost and latency: the 8-11 minute setup, the cost per iteration, the 24-iteration ceiling and the 50/35/15 depth-band split, each checked against the measured runs in SPEC/review/, SPEC/review-archive/ and the live playbook logs.\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/455-expansive-depth-integration && grep -rn \\\"8-11\\\\|8–11\\\\|8 to 11\\\\|11 minutes\\\\|eight minute\\\\|minutes of setup\\\" .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop .skilled/commands/deep specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode 2>/dev/null | head -20\"}"},"time":{"start":1795137742151,"end":1795137746012}}}}
