{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1795138120782,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"id":"prt_1f691ec4e001UnIMhdJviDLH2z","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f691e4f9001FY7OfIguvv33fq","type":"step-start","snapshot":"c1268bcdd28b54cf4d76b8cbdfb00f54e2a1b04c"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795138128211,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"partID":"prt_1f691f23c001GBFZWPhEKrFBDd","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f691e4f9001FY7OfIguvv33fq","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a86da75fef5dd141","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 7: Frontier node F-2-008 — per-node dashboard detail across both reducers\n\n## Focus\n\nFrontier node F-2-008 (depth 2, child of F-1-002): do the research and review dashboards expose per-frontier-node budget spend and visit counts, or only lineage-wide iteration totals?\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **The review reducer already computes a per-node `visits` count; the research reducer drops it from its projection.** `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:1972-2008` builds a visit array and puts `visits: records.length` on each projected node, while `deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2453-2475` projects only `nodeId, parentId, depth, scope, question, status, reason, iteration`. The research dashboard can therefore report a total (`Revisits` at `:3108`) but cannot print per-node detail, even though `frontierNodeVisits(nodeId, iterationRecords)` is already defined beside the projection (`:2499`). One concrete change: add `visits` (and `coveredDimensions` where the review reducer has it) to the research projection and print a per-node table. Value: three single-node passes stop looking like three passes on one node. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:1972] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2453] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2499]\n\n2. **The frontier dashboard section has already drifted between the two reducers.** Review renders `## 10B. FRONTIER` with size, pruned, revisits, every band budget and an active-node line (`runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520-2545`); research renders `## 6B. FRONTIER` with size, pruned, revisits and band budgets but no active node (`deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` section 6B). One concrete change: extract one `renderFrontierDashboardSection(frontier, iterationRecords)` helper and call it from both reducers. Value: one dashboard contract, and a new field lands in both places at once. Effort M. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520] [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:108]\n\n3. **The split has confused even this study's own scope line: it names `deep-review/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`, a path that does not exist.** The review reducer lives at `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`; the research reducer lives at `deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`; there is no `deep-review/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` (`deep-review/scripts/` holds only the pivot, a README, a contract renderer, capabilities and tests). One concrete change: move the research reducer to `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` (or a shared `runtime/lib/deep-loop/frontier-projection` module) so the two loops name one path. Value: removes a wrong path from the operating model and makes the shared helper above natural. Effort M. Risk medium (workflow paths and tests must move together). [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml:1149] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Claiming both dashboards hide band spend: both print the three bands and their allotments; the missing piece is per-node detail and the active-node line.\n- Adding a new registry file: the projection is the right carrier; it already has the visit function.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Searching for a review-side per-node table in the committed review dashboard: it reports the same aggregate size and revisits, so the per-node table is absent on both sides, not only in research.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"budget spend\" could mean lineage-wide or per-band. Read as per-band and per-node; the bands are already printed, the nodes are not.\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\n- Missing dependencies: the nonexistent path in the scope line is itself evidence, not a missing file to fetch.\n- Partial success: none.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:1972,2520`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2453,2499,3108`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml:1149`\n- `specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:108`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: dashboard per-node detail (frontier node F-2-008)\n- Questions answered: yes, the node question: both dashboards show only aggregate frontier state, and the two renderers have drifted.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: diffing the two frontier projection functions found a one-field gap that explains the dashboard difference and costs almost nothing to close.\n- What did not work and why: looking for a per-node table in the review dashboard found none; the review reducer has the data but does not render it either.\n- What I would do differently: read both dashboard renderers side by side before writing the first dashboard finding.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nClose F-2-008 as answered; the frontier is then terminal (F-1-006 was closed by the depth-1 band budget). Next: Q1 restraint (concrete deletions), then Q5 design questions and Q6 prior art.\n","path":"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 7: Frontier node F-2-008 — per-node dashboard detail across both reducers\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nFrontier node F-2-008 (depth 2, child of F-1-002): do the research and review dashboards expose per-frontier-node budget spend and visit counts, or only lineage-wide iteration totals?\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. **The review reducer already computes a per-node `visits` count; the research reducer drops it from its projection.** `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:1972-2008` builds a visit array and puts `visits: records.length` on each projected node, while `deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2453-2475` projects only `nodeId, parentId, depth, scope, question, status, reason, iteration`. The research dashboard can therefore report a total (`Revisits` at `:3108`) but cannot print per-node detail, even though `frontierNodeVisits(nodeId, iterationRecords)` is already defined beside the projection (`:2499`). One concrete change: add `visits` (and `coveredDimensions` where the review reducer has it) to the research projection and print a per-node table. Value: three single-node passes stop looking like three passes on one node. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:1972] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2453] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2499]\\n\\n2. **The frontier dashboard section has already drifted between the two reducers.** Review renders `## 10B. FRONTIER` with size, pruned, revisits, every band budget and an active-node line (`runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520-2545`); research renders `## 6B. FRONTIER` with size, pruned, revisits and band budgets but no active node (`deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` section 6B). One concrete change: extract one `renderFrontierDashboardSection(frontier, iterationRecords)` helper and call it from both reducers. Value: one dashboard contract, and a new field lands in both places at once. Effort M. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520] [SOURCE: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:108]\\n\\n3. **The split has confused even this study's own scope line: it names `deep-review/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`, a path that does not exist.** The review reducer lives at `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`; the research reducer lives at `deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs`; there is no `deep-review/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` (`deep-review/scripts/` holds only the pivot, a README, a contract renderer, capabilities and tests). One concrete change: move the research reducer to `runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs` (or a shared `runtime/lib/deep-loop/frontier-projection` module) so the two loops name one path. Value: removes a wrong path from the operating model and makes the shared helper above natural. Effort M. Risk medium (workflow paths and tests must move together). [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml:1149] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Claiming both dashboards hide band spend: both print the three bands and their allotments; the missing piece is per-node detail and the active-node line.\\n- Adding a new registry file: the projection is the right carrier; it already has the visit function.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Searching for a review-side per-node table in the committed review dashboard: it reports the same aggregate size and revisits, so the per-node table is absent on both sides, not only in research.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"budget spend\\\" could mean lineage-wide or per-band. Read as per-band and per-node; the bands are already printed, the nodes are not.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\\n- Missing dependencies: the nonexistent path in the scope line is itself evidence, not a missing file to fetch.\\n- Partial success: none.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:1972,2520`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2453,2499,3108`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review.yaml:1149`\\n- `specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/deep-research-dashboard.md:108`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: dashboard per-node detail (frontier node F-2-008)\\n- Questions answered: yes, the node question: both dashboards show only aggregate frontier state, and the two renderers have drifted.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: diffing the two frontier projection functions found a one-field gap that explains the dashboard difference and costs almost nothing to close.\\n- What did not work and why: looking for a per-node table in the review dashboard found none; the review reducer has the data but does not render it either.\\n- What I would do differently: read both dashboard renderers side by side before writing the first dashboard finding.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nClose F-2-008 as answered; the frontier is then terminal (F-1-006 was closed by the depth-1 band budget). Next: Q1 restraint (concrete deletions), then Q5 design questions and Q6 prior art.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-007.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795138122300,"end":1795138128211}}}}
