{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795138177461,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"partID":"prt_1f692b2a8002mUfUKX3RV53z26","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f6929ec4001jDJHwMIwXc6bGV","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-90fa93068f386589","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 8: Q1 restraint — concrete deletions that keep every promise\n\n## Focus\n\nQ1 restraint: what can be deleted, merged or collapsed across `frontier-admit.cjs`, both `reduce-state.cjs` reducers, `convergence.cjs`, `fanout-run.cjs` and the two workflow YAMLs while every promise stays kept?\n\n**Edge case, recorded:** the strategy's Next Focus carried this lineage's own iteration-7 closing instruction (\"Close F-2-008 as answered…\") verbatim, because the reducer reads the latest leaf `Recommended Next Focus` when the frontier is terminal. The loop is therefore working Q1 on the next open question rather than following that instruction. This is a real defect and is recorded for the design-question iteration; it does not change this iteration's scope.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **`dispatch-iteration.cjs` builds half its executors by hand and half through `buildLineageCommand`, and the hand-built half is where the parity bugs live.** `runClaude` (`:354-453`) and `runCodex` (`:919-995`) construct argv inline, while `runLineageBuiltin`, `runResearchOpencode`, `runDeepseekHarness` and the review opencode handler call the shared builder (`:478, :540, :599, :793`). One concrete change: route claude and codex through `buildLineageCommand` plus the existing audit wrapper and delete the two inline builders, which removes roughly 300 lines and the `acceptEdits` drift found in iteration 5 in the same edit. Value: the largest single deletion in the runtime, and it closes a live permission defect. Effort M. Risk medium (the claude `--claude-config-dir` and codex stdin paths must be preserved in the adapter). [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:354] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:919] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:478]\n\n2. **Six of the eight executor branches in each YAML are the same command, and their notes restate the capability matrix.** `deep-research.yaml:1762-1837` and the review YAML carry eight `if_cli_*` blocks; six differ only in `--executor <kind>`, and the notes under them repeat `EXECUTOR_KIND_FLAG_SUPPORT`. One concrete change: one `if_cli` branch with `--executor \"{config.executor.kind}\"`, keep the claude config-dir and dsh spec-folder special cases, delete the notes. Value: about 100 lines per YAML, and the matrix stays the only place capabilities are stated. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1762] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1819] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts:112]\n\n3. **The research YAML writes the same frontier seed step twice and the same fan-out command twice.** `step_frontier_seed` appears at `:395-415` (fan-out) and `:816-834` (phase_init) with the same command and different `skip_when`; `step_fanout_dry_run_preview` (`:300-313`) repeats `step_fanout_spawn_cli`'s command (`:439-452`) verbatim apart from `--preview`; and `step_reject_extend_with_fanout` (`:372-382`) plus `step_reject_frontier_caps_with_fanout` (`:384-393`) are two copies of one guard shape. One concrete change: one seed step with a branch on `config.fanout`, one spawn command with a `--preview` modifier, and one \"usage errors refused before spawn\" step. Value: about 50 lines removed and one place to edit each rule. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:395] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:816] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:300] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:372]\n\n4. **The frontier marker grammar and the frontier dashboard renderer are implemented twice, in different trees.** `convergence.cjs:1360-1364` builds `frontierMarkerPattern`, and `deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2485-2496` re-implements the same regex in `frontierNodeVisitRecords`; the review reducer renders `10B. FRONTIER` with an active-node line while the research reducer renders `6B. FRONTIER` without it. One concrete change: one shared `runtime/lib/deep-loop/frontier-projection` module exporting the marker, `visits`, `pickFocus` and `renderDashboardSection`, imported by both reducers and convergence. Value: one grammar and one dashboard, and the review reducer's visit logic becomes the research reducer's for free. Effort M. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs:1360] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2485] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520]\n\n5. **Two small copies remain: the Flash effort pin and the `native` fan-out adapter.** `fanout-run.cjs:2650-2668` mirrors `executor-config.ts:328-337` (`isFlashMaxPinnedModel`, `pinReasoningEffortForModel`), and `native` is a fan-out adapter (`:3558`) that cannot start on OpenCode v2 (`:2640-2645`) while `dispatch-iteration.cjs` already rejects it (`:64`). One concrete change: import the pin, delete the mirror, and remove `native` from the adapter table in favour of the command host. Value: about 45 lines removed and one less version-dependent path. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:2650] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts:328] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:3558] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:2640]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Deleting `frontier-admit.cjs` as a whole: it is shared by both reducers and both loops, so it is the one place the frontier rules already live; the duplication is around it, not inside it.\n- Collapsing the two reducers into one file: the review reducer carries dimension coverage and the research reducer carries novelty questions; the shared part is the frontier projection, not the whole reducer.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Searching for dead `if_cli_*` branches: all eight are reachable, so the deletion is a merge, not a dead-code removal.\n- Looking for a shared YAML macro system: the workflow format has no include mechanism, so duplication must be collapsed at the step level, not by templating.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"deleted\" could mean lines or concepts. Both are counted where they apply; the line counts are approximate, the concepts are exact.\n- Contradictory evidence: none; the duplicate code is textual.\n- Missing dependencies: none.\n- Partial success: none.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:354,478,919`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:2640,2650,3558`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs:1360`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2485`\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts:112,328`\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:300,372,395,816,1762,1819`\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: Q1 restraint\n- Questions answered: Q1: five concrete deletions, about 500 lines plus one shared module, with the live claude permission defect closed by the largest one.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: counting the built-by-hand versus built-by-adapter handlers turned \"parity\" into a deletion with a defect fix attached.\n- What did not work and why: looking for dead branches found none; the duplication is live code on both paths, which is why it drifted.\n- What I would do differently: start the restraint pass from the largest files' function tables, where duplication is visible as pairs of function names.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nQ5 design questions (research stuck scoping, lineage completion for older runs, pivot admission), then Q6 prior art, then synthesis.\n","path":"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 8: Q1 restraint — concrete deletions that keep every promise\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nQ1 restraint: what can be deleted, merged or collapsed across `frontier-admit.cjs`, both `reduce-state.cjs` reducers, `convergence.cjs`, `fanout-run.cjs` and the two workflow YAMLs while every promise stays kept?\\n\\n**Edge case, recorded:** the strategy's Next Focus carried this lineage's own iteration-7 closing instruction (\\\"Close F-2-008 as answered…\\\") verbatim, because the reducer reads the latest leaf `Recommended Next Focus` when the frontier is terminal. The loop is therefore working Q1 on the next open question rather than following that instruction. This is a real defect and is recorded for the design-question iteration; it does not change this iteration's scope.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. **`dispatch-iteration.cjs` builds half its executors by hand and half through `buildLineageCommand`, and the hand-built half is where the parity bugs live.** `runClaude` (`:354-453`) and `runCodex` (`:919-995`) construct argv inline, while `runLineageBuiltin`, `runResearchOpencode`, `runDeepseekHarness` and the review opencode handler call the shared builder (`:478, :540, :599, :793`). One concrete change: route claude and codex through `buildLineageCommand` plus the existing audit wrapper and delete the two inline builders, which removes roughly 300 lines and the `acceptEdits` drift found in iteration 5 in the same edit. Value: the largest single deletion in the runtime, and it closes a live permission defect. Effort M. Risk medium (the claude `--claude-config-dir` and codex stdin paths must be preserved in the adapter). [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:354] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:919] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:478]\\n\\n2. **Six of the eight executor branches in each YAML are the same command, and their notes restate the capability matrix.** `deep-research.yaml:1762-1837` and the review YAML carry eight `if_cli_*` blocks; six differ only in `--executor <kind>`, and the notes under them repeat `EXECUTOR_KIND_FLAG_SUPPORT`. One concrete change: one `if_cli` branch with `--executor \\\"{config.executor.kind}\\\"`, keep the claude config-dir and dsh spec-folder special cases, delete the notes. Value: about 100 lines per YAML, and the matrix stays the only place capabilities are stated. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1762] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:1819] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts:112]\\n\\n3. **The research YAML writes the same frontier seed step twice and the same fan-out command twice.** `step_frontier_seed` appears at `:395-415` (fan-out) and `:816-834` (phase_init) with the same command and different `skip_when`; `step_fanout_dry_run_preview` (`:300-313`) repeats `step_fanout_spawn_cli`'s command (`:439-452`) verbatim apart from `--preview`; and `step_reject_extend_with_fanout` (`:372-382`) plus `step_reject_frontier_caps_with_fanout` (`:384-393`) are two copies of one guard shape. One concrete change: one seed step with a branch on `config.fanout`, one spawn command with a `--preview` modifier, and one \\\"usage errors refused before spawn\\\" step. Value: about 50 lines removed and one place to edit each rule. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:395] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:816] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:300] [SOURCE: .skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:372]\\n\\n4. **The frontier marker grammar and the frontier dashboard renderer are implemented twice, in different trees.** `convergence.cjs:1360-1364` builds `frontierMarkerPattern`, and `deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2485-2496` re-implements the same regex in `frontierNodeVisitRecords`; the review reducer renders `10B. FRONTIER` with an active-node line while the research reducer renders `6B. FRONTIER` without it. One concrete change: one shared `runtime/lib/deep-loop/frontier-projection` module exporting the marker, `visits`, `pickFocus` and `renderDashboardSection`, imported by both reducers and convergence. Value: one grammar and one dashboard, and the review reducer's visit logic becomes the research reducer's for free. Effort M. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs:1360] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2485] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520]\\n\\n5. **Two small copies remain: the Flash effort pin and the `native` fan-out adapter.** `fanout-run.cjs:2650-2668` mirrors `executor-config.ts:328-337` (`isFlashMaxPinnedModel`, `pinReasoningEffortForModel`), and `native` is a fan-out adapter (`:3558`) that cannot start on OpenCode v2 (`:2640-2645`) while `dispatch-iteration.cjs` already rejects it (`:64`). One concrete change: import the pin, delete the mirror, and remove `native` from the adapter table in favour of the command host. Value: about 45 lines removed and one less version-dependent path. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:2650] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts:328] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:3558] [SOURCE: .skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:2640]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Deleting `frontier-admit.cjs` as a whole: it is shared by both reducers and both loops, so it is the one place the frontier rules already live; the duplication is around it, not inside it.\\n- Collapsing the two reducers into one file: the review reducer carries dimension coverage and the research reducer carries novelty questions; the shared part is the frontier projection, not the whole reducer.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Searching for dead `if_cli_*` branches: all eight are reachable, so the deletion is a merge, not a dead-code removal.\\n- Looking for a shared YAML macro system: the workflow format has no include mechanism, so duplication must be collapsed at the step level, not by templating.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"deleted\\\" could mean lines or concepts. Both are counted where they apply; the line counts are approximate, the concepts are exact.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none; the duplicate code is textual.\\n- Missing dependencies: none.\\n- Partial success: none.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/dispatch-iteration.cjs:354,478,919`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/fanout-run.cjs:2640,2650,3558`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/convergence.cjs:1360`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2520`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-research/scripts/reduce-state.cjs:2485`\\n- `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts:112,328`\\n- `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-research.yaml:300,372,395,816,1762,1819`\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: Q1 restraint\\n- Questions answered: Q1: five concrete deletions, about 500 lines plus one shared module, with the live claude permission defect closed by the largest one.\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: counting the built-by-hand versus built-by-adapter handlers turned \\\"parity\\\" into a deletion with a defect fix attached.\\n- What did not work and why: looking for dead branches found none; the duplication is live code on both paths, which is why it drifted.\\n- What I would do differently: start the restraint pass from the largest files' function tables, where duplication is visible as pairs of function names.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nQ5 design questions (research stuck scoping, lineage completion for older runs, pivot admission), then Q6 prior art, then synthesis.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-008.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795138171560,"end":1795138177461}}}}
