{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1795138244935,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"id":"prt_1f693d147001uThtnixyJADZ0q","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f693c7dc001k4UIMYt5A0UV8D","type":"step-start","snapshot":"5f0f61b946e368f18f26f9e3a40c5f4be8e8176e"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1795138250349,"sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","part":{"partID":"prt_1f693d69c001ONA7llZaREVLUU","sessionID":"ses_e09762d57ffewQMol0oGMct7ei","messageID":"msg_1f693c7dc001k4UIMYt5A0UV8D","type":"tool","id":"chatcmpl-tool-917384dfcc910d63","tool":"write","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"# Iteration 10: Q6 — prior art for frontier growth, stuck detection, stop rules and fan-out\n\n## Focus\n\nHow do other agentic research and code-review systems handle frontier growth, stuck detection, stop rules and multi-executor fan-out?\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **Anthropic's production research system is the same orchestrator-worker shape, with explicit effort-scaling rules and checkpointed resume.** The lead agent spawns 3-5 subagents in parallel, each with an objective, an output format, tool guidance and task boundaries; effort is scaled by query complexity (simple fact-finding: 1 agent, 3-10 tool calls; direct comparison: 2-4 subagents, 10-15 calls each; complex research: more than 10 subagents). It reports multi-agent runs at about 15× the tokens of a chat, and names synchronous subagent execution as a bottleneck. The concrete transfer: add an effort-scaling table to `research.md` and `review.md` (simple / comparison / complex → lineages × iterations × tool calls), which this runtime lacks; its floors are per-kind, not per-complexity. Value: an operator sizes a run without arithmetic. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system]\n\n2. **LangChain's Open Deep Research exposes per-researcher limits and a published cost table.** Its supervisor-researcher graph caps researcher iterations, runs researchers in parallel, and its README reports total cost and tokens per benchmark run ($45.98 / 58.0M tokens for the default, $187.09 / 138.9M for Claude Sonnet 4). The concrete transfer: put a `budget` block on each lineage (`maxIterations`, `maxToolCalls`, estimated cost) and print it before spawn, so the operator sees the price the way LangChain's table shows it. Value: makes cost a first-class parameter instead of a post-hoc surprise. Effort S/M. Risk low. [SOURCE: https://github.com/langchain-ai/open_deep_research]\n\n3. **Tree of Thoughts makes search explicit: generate candidates, self-evaluate, then expand or backtrack.** ToT's contribution is deliberate search over coherent thoughts with evaluation and backtracking, not a fixed left-to-right chain. This runtime's frontier grows by structural admission only (relevance, dedupe, depth, fan-out, budget, cycle) and its focus order is shallowest → least visited → oldest; neither step scores expected value. The concrete transfer: add an optional `priority` (or `expectedValue`) field to `frontier_proposal` and rank focus by it after depth, so a high-value child is not queued behind an older low-value one. Value: growth follows value, not arrival order. Effort M. Risk medium (a model-authored priority must not be trusted over the caps). [SOURCE: https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601]\n\n4. **STORM gets breadth from distinct perspectives, not from more questions in one voice.** STORM discovers diverse perspectives and simulates writers who pose questions from each, then curates an outline; its evaluation attributes coverage gains to that multi-perspective asking. This runtime's seed asks for 3-6 sub-questions that \"together cover the topic\", with no perspective requirement and a text-based dedupe key. The concrete transfer: require each seed question to name a distinct perspective or role and dedupe on that perspective, which also gives the proposal admission a semantic axis that plain text comparison lacks. Value: less near-duplicate seeding, broader first-pass coverage. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14207]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Copying Anthropic's synchronous lead-agent loop: this runtime already has the fan-out pool and per-lineage state, so the transferable part is the effort-scaling table and the checkpoint/resume discipline, not the topology.\n- Adopting a full MCTS search (LATS-style) for the frontier: the measured runs are 10-24 iterations, too shallow for tree search to pay for its branching overhead; a priority field is the cheap 80%.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Looking for a published stuck-detection rule in these systems: Anthropic handles failure with retries and checkpoints, LangChain with iteration caps, ToT with evaluation; none publishes a per-node stuck counter, so this runtime's per-node counter is ahead of the public prior art and should be kept.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: \"frontier growth\" could mean breadth (more depth-1 nodes) or depth (children). Both are covered: STORM for breadth, ToT for depth ordering.\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\n- Missing dependencies: no access to the systems' internal code; the public engineering write-ups and papers are the evidence.\n- Partial success: none.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system\n- https://github.com/langchain-ai/open_deep_research\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14207\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 1.0\n- Questions addressed: Q6 prior art\n- Questions answered: Q6: four transferable practices (effort-scaling table, per-lineage budget/cost, proposal priority, perspective-based seeding).\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: reading the production write-up beside this runtime's own seed and admission rules made the missing effort-scaling table obvious.\n- What did not work and why: searching for a published per-node stuck rule found none; the local per-node counter is already the stronger design.\n- What I would do differently: fetch the systems' configuration docs, not only their READMEs, if a future pass needs exact parameter names.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nSynthesis: rank the recommendations by value over effort, merge duplicates, and state the one change that buys the most for the least.\n","path":"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-010.md"},"output":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-010.md","title":"write","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Created file successfully: specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-010.md"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"content\": \"# Iteration 10: Q6 — prior art for frontier growth, stuck detection, stop rules and fan-out\\n\\n## Focus\\n\\nHow do other agentic research and code-review systems handle frontier growth, stuck detection, stop rules and multi-executor fan-out?\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n1. **Anthropic's production research system is the same orchestrator-worker shape, with explicit effort-scaling rules and checkpointed resume.** The lead agent spawns 3-5 subagents in parallel, each with an objective, an output format, tool guidance and task boundaries; effort is scaled by query complexity (simple fact-finding: 1 agent, 3-10 tool calls; direct comparison: 2-4 subagents, 10-15 calls each; complex research: more than 10 subagents). It reports multi-agent runs at about 15× the tokens of a chat, and names synchronous subagent execution as a bottleneck. The concrete transfer: add an effort-scaling table to `research.md` and `review.md` (simple / comparison / complex → lineages × iterations × tool calls), which this runtime lacks; its floors are per-kind, not per-complexity. Value: an operator sizes a run without arithmetic. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system]\\n\\n2. **LangChain's Open Deep Research exposes per-researcher limits and a published cost table.** Its supervisor-researcher graph caps researcher iterations, runs researchers in parallel, and its README reports total cost and tokens per benchmark run ($45.98 / 58.0M tokens for the default, $187.09 / 138.9M for Claude Sonnet 4). The concrete transfer: put a `budget` block on each lineage (`maxIterations`, `maxToolCalls`, estimated cost) and print it before spawn, so the operator sees the price the way LangChain's table shows it. Value: makes cost a first-class parameter instead of a post-hoc surprise. Effort S/M. Risk low. [SOURCE: https://github.com/langchain-ai/open_deep_research]\\n\\n3. **Tree of Thoughts makes search explicit: generate candidates, self-evaluate, then expand or backtrack.** ToT's contribution is deliberate search over coherent thoughts with evaluation and backtracking, not a fixed left-to-right chain. This runtime's frontier grows by structural admission only (relevance, dedupe, depth, fan-out, budget, cycle) and its focus order is shallowest → least visited → oldest; neither step scores expected value. The concrete transfer: add an optional `priority` (or `expectedValue`) field to `frontier_proposal` and rank focus by it after depth, so a high-value child is not queued behind an older low-value one. Value: growth follows value, not arrival order. Effort M. Risk medium (a model-authored priority must not be trusted over the caps). [SOURCE: https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601]\\n\\n4. **STORM gets breadth from distinct perspectives, not from more questions in one voice.** STORM discovers diverse perspectives and simulates writers who pose questions from each, then curates an outline; its evaluation attributes coverage gains to that multi-perspective asking. This runtime's seed asks for 3-6 sub-questions that \\\"together cover the topic\\\", with no perspective requirement and a text-based dedupe key. The concrete transfer: require each seed question to name a distinct perspective or role and dedupe on that perspective, which also gives the proposal admission a semantic axis that plain text comparison lacks. Value: less near-duplicate seeding, broader first-pass coverage. Effort S. Risk low. [SOURCE: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14207]\\n\\n## Ruled Out\\n\\n- Copying Anthropic's synchronous lead-agent loop: this runtime already has the fan-out pool and per-lineage state, so the transferable part is the effort-scaling table and the checkpoint/resume discipline, not the topology.\\n- Adopting a full MCTS search (LATS-style) for the frontier: the measured runs are 10-24 iterations, too shallow for tree search to pay for its branching overhead; a priority field is the cheap 80%.\\n\\n## Dead Ends\\n\\n- Looking for a published stuck-detection rule in these systems: Anthropic handles failure with retries and checkpoints, LangChain with iteration caps, ToT with evaluation; none publishes a per-node stuck counter, so this runtime's per-node counter is ahead of the public prior art and should be kept.\\n\\n## Edge Cases\\n\\n- Ambiguous input: \\\"frontier growth\\\" could mean breadth (more depth-1 nodes) or depth (children). Both are covered: STORM for breadth, ToT for depth ordering.\\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\\n- Missing dependencies: no access to the systems' internal code; the public engineering write-ups and papers are the evidence.\\n- Partial success: none.\\n\\n## Sources Consulted\\n\\n- https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system\\n- https://github.com/langchain-ai/open_deep_research\\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.10601\\n- https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.14207\\n\\n## Assessment\\n\\n- New information ratio: 1.0\\n- Questions addressed: Q6 prior art\\n- Questions answered: Q6: four transferable practices (effort-scaling table, per-lineage budget/cost, proposal priority, perspective-based seeding).\\n\\n## Reflection\\n\\n- What worked and why: reading the production write-up beside this runtime's own seed and admission rules made the missing effort-scaling table obvious.\\n- What did not work and why: searching for a published per-node stuck rule found none; the local per-node counter is already the stronger design.\\n- What I would do differently: fetch the systems' configuration docs, not only their READMEs, if a future pass needs exact parameter names.\\n\\n## Recommended Next Focus\\n\\nSynthesis: rank the recommendations by value over effort, merge duplicates, and state the one change that buys the most for the least.\\n\", \"path\": \"specs/system-deep-loop/loop-architecture/002-graph-engineering/007-expansive-depth-mode/research/lineages/deepseek/iterations/iteration-010.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1795138246300,"end":1795138250349}}}}
